Page 13611 Cikán: Found match for regex: =
Page 13611 Cikán: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|cs|cikán}}

===Etymology===
Possibly from an {{der|cs|grc|-}} term meaning "untouchable"; compare the modern {{cog|el|-}} designations {{m|el|Τσιγγάνος|Τσιγγάνοι}}, {{m|el|Αθίγγανος|Αθίγγανοι}}.<ref>2004, Viorel Achim, ''The Roma in Romanian History'' (Bucharest), page 9</ref><ref>2007, Jean-Pierre Liégeois, ''Roma In Europe'', page 17</ref><ref>1993, ''Struggling for Ethnic Identity: The Gypsies of Hungary'' (published by Human Rights Watch), page 1</ref> Cognates include {{cog|hu|cigány}}, {{cog|en|tzigane}}, {{cog|it|zigano}}, {{cog|pt|cigano}}, {{cog|pl|Cygan}}, {{cog|ru|цыган}}, {{cog|de|Zigeuner}}, and {{cog|es|cíngaro}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{cs-IPA}}
* {{hyphenation|cs|Ci|kán}}

===Noun===
{{cs-noun|m-an|f=Cikánka}}

# a [[Gypsy]], a [[Romani]]
#: {{syn|cs|Rom}}

====Usage notes====
* The term ''Cikán''/''cikán'' is often intentionally [[derogatory]] and is therefore considered [[racist]] by some. Consequently, careful speakers use the term {{m|cs|Rom}} instead. ''Cikán'' is still the prevailing term in informal Czech and in historical usage, however.

====Declension====
{{cs-ndecl|m.an.nomplové:i}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|sk|cigán}}

===References===
<references/>

===Further reading===
* {{R:cs:KNLA}}
* {{R:cs:PSJC|cikán}}
* {{R:cs:SSJC|cikán}}

{{C|cs|People}}
